:null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:21","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:22","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:23","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:24","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:25","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:26","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:27","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:28","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:29","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:30","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:31","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:32","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:33","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:34","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:35","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:36","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:37","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:38","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null},{"document_id":"scierc:train:39","principal":"public","granted_at":0,"revoked_at":null}],"chunks":[{"id":1,"document_id":"scierc:train:0","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":153,"text":"English is shown to be trans-context-free on the basis of coordinations of the respectively type that involve strictly syntactic cross-serial agreement ."},{"id":2,"document_id":"scierc:train:1","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":237,"text":"The agreement in question involves number in nouns and reflexive pronouns and is syntactic rather than semantic in nature because grammatical number in English , like grammatical gender in languages such as French , is partly arbitrary ."},{"id":3,"document_id":"scierc:train:2","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":497,"text":"The formal proof , which makes crucial use of the Interchange Lemma of Ogden et al. , is so constructed as to be valid even if English is presumed to contain grammatical sentences in which respectively operates across a pair of coordinate phrases one of whose members has fewer conjuncts than the other ; it thus goes through whatever the facts may be regarding constructions with unequal numbers of conjuncts in the scope of respectively , whereas other arguments have foundered on this problem ."},{"id":4,"document_id":"scierc:train:3","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":111,"text":"In this paper , a novel method to learn the intrinsic object structure for robust visual tracking is proposed ."},{"id":5,"document_id":"scierc:train:4","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":134,"text":"The basic assumption is that the parameterized object state lies on a low dimensional manifold and can be learned from training data ."},{"id":6,"document_id":"scierc:train:5","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":245,"text":"Based on this assumption , firstly we derived the dimensionality reduction and density estimation algorithm for unsupervised learning of object intrinsic representation , the obtained non-rigid part of object state reduces even to 2 dimensions ."},{"id":7,"document_id":"scierc:train:6","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":92,"text":"Secondly the dynamical model is derived and trained based on this intrinsic representation ."},{"id":8,"document_id":"scierc:train:7","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":99,"text":"Thirdly the learned intrinsic object structure is integrated into a particle-filter style tracker ."},{"id":9,"document_id":"scierc:train:8","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":202,"text":"We will show that this intrinsic object representation has some interesting properties and based on which the newly derived dynamical model makes particle-filter style tracker more robust and reliable ."},{"id":10,"document_id":"scierc:train:9","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":207,"text":"Experiments show that the learned tracker performs much better than existing trackers on the tracking of complex non-rigid motions such as fish twisting with self-occlusion and large inter-frame lip motion ."},{"id":11,"document_id":"scierc:train:10","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":85,"text":"The proposed method also has the potential to solve other type of tracking problems ."},{"id":12,"document_id":"scierc:train:11","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":197,"text":"In this paper , we present a digital signal processor ( DSP ) implementation of real-time statistical voice conversion ( VC ) for silent speech enhancement and electrolaryngeal speech enhancement ."},{"id":13,"document_id":"scierc:train:12","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":144,"text":"As a silent speech interface , we focus on non-audible murmur ( NAM ) , which can be used in situations where audible speech is not acceptable ."},{"id":14,"document_id":"scierc:train:13","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":136,"text":"Electrolaryngeal speech is one of the typical types of alaryngeal speech produced by an alternative speaking method for laryngectomees ."},{"id":15,"document_id":"scierc:train:14","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":97,"text":"However , the sound quality of NAM and electrolaryngeal speech suffers from lack of naturalness ."},{"id":16,"document_id":"scierc:train:15","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":171,"text":"VC has proven to be one of the promising approaches to address this problem , and it has been successfully implemented on devices with sufficient computational resources ."},{"id":17,"document_id":"scierc:train:16","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":142,"text":"An implementation on devices that are highly portable but have limited computational resources would greatly contribute to its practical use ."},{"id":18,"document_id":"scierc:train:17","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":58,"text":"In this paper we further implement real-time VC on a DSP ."},{"id":19,"document_id":"scierc:train:18","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":267,"text":"To implement the two speech enhancement systems based on real-time VC , one from NAM to a whispered voice and the other from electrolaryngeal speech to a natural voice , we propose several methods for reducing computational cost while preserving conversion accuracy ."},{"id":20,"document_id":"scierc:train:19","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":119,"text":"We conduct experimental evaluations and show that real-time VC is capable of running on a DSP with little degradation ."},{"id":21,"document_id":"scierc:train:20","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":187,"text":"We propose a method that automatically generates paraphrase sets from seed sentences to be used as reference sets in objective machine translation evaluation measures like BLEU and NIST ."},{"id":22,"document_id":"scierc:train:21","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":405,"text":"We measured the quality of the paraphrases produced in an experiment , i.e. , ( i ) their grammaticality : at least 99 % correct sentences ; ( ii ) their equivalence in meaning : at least 96 % correct paraphrases either by meaning equivalence or entailment ; and , ( iii ) the amount of internal lexical and syntactical variation in a set of paraphrases : slightly superior to that of hand-produced sets ."},{"id":23,"document_id":"scierc:train:22","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":111,"text":"The paraphrase sets produced by this method thus seem adequate as reference sets to be used for MT evaluation ."},{"id":24,"document_id":"scierc:train:23","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":88,"text":"Graph unification remains the most expensive part of unification-based grammar parsing ."},{"id":25,"document_id":"scierc:train:24","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":121,"text":"We focus on one speed-up element in the design of unification algorithms : avoidance of copying of unmodified subgraphs ."},{"id":26,"document_id":"scierc:train:25","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":215,"text":"We propose a method of attaining such a design through a method of structure-sharing which avoids log ( d ) overheads often associated with structure-sharing of graphs without any use of costly dependency pointers ."},{"id":27,"document_id":"scierc:train:26","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":232,"text":"The proposed scheme eliminates redundant copying while maintaining the quasi-destructive scheme 's ability to avoid over copying and early copying combined with its ability to handle cyclic structures without algorithmic additions ."},{"id":28,"document_id":"scierc:train:27","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":123,"text":"We describe a novel technique and implemented system for constructing a subcategorization dictionary from textual corpora ."},{"id":29,"document_id":"scierc:train:28","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":132,"text":"Each dictionary entry encodes the relative frequency of occurrence of a comprehensive set of subcategorization classes for English ."},{"id":30,"document_id":"scierc:train:29","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":263,"text":"An initial experiment , on a sample of 14 verbs which exhibit multiple complementation patterns , demonstrates that the technique achieves accuracy comparable to previous approaches , which are all limited to a highly restricted set of subcategorization classes ."},{"id":31,"document_id":"scierc:train:30","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":136,"text":"We also demonstrate that a subcategorization dictionary built with the system improves the accuracy of a parser by an appreciable amount"},{"id":32,"document_id":"scierc:train:31","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":119,"text":"The introduction of prior knowledge has greatly enhanced numerous purely low-level driven image processing algorithms ."},{"id":33,"document_id":"scierc:train:32","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":72,"text":"In this work , we focus on the problem of non-rigid image registration ."},{"id":34,"document_id":"scierc:train:33","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":146,"text":"A number of powerful registration criteria have been developed in the last decade , most prominently the criterion of maximum mutual information ."},{"id":35,"document_id":"scierc:train:34","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":127,"text":"Although this criterion provides for good registration results in many applications , it remains a purely low-level criterion ."},{"id":36,"document_id":"scierc:train:35","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":166,"text":"As a consequence , registration results will deteriorate once this low-level information is corrupted , due to noise , partial occlusions or missing image structure ."},{"id":37,"document_id":"scierc:train:36","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":185,"text":"In this paper , we will develop a Bayesian framework that allows to impose statistically learned prior knowledge about the joint intensity distribution into image registration methods ."},{"id":38,"document_id":"scierc:train:37","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":160,"text":"The prior is given by a kernel density estimate on the space of joint intensity distributions computed from a representative set of pre-registered image pairs ."},{"id":39,"document_id":"scierc:train:38","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":136,"text":"This nonparametric prior accurately models previously learned intensity relations between various image modalities and slice locations ."},{"id":40,"document_id":"scierc:train:39","parent_id":null,"level":0,"span_start":0,"span_end":227,"text":"Experimental results demonstrate that the resulting registration process is more robust to missing low-level information as it favors intensity correspondences statistically consistent with the learned intensity distributions ."}]},"extraction":[{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:1","subject":"nouns","predicate":"Conjunction","object":"reflexive pronouns"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:1","subject":"grammatical gender","predicate":"Feature-of","object":"languages"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:1","subject":"French","predicate":"Hyponym-of","object":"languages"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:3","subject":"method","predicate":"Used-for","object":"intrinsic object structure"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:3","subject":"intrinsic object structure","predicate":"Used-for","object":"robust visual tracking"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:4","subject":"low dimensional manifold","predicate":"Feature-of","object":"parameterized object state"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:5","subject":"dimensionality reduction and density estimation algorithm","predicate":"Used-for","object":"unsupervised learning of object intrinsic representation"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:6","subject":"intrinsic representation","predicate":"Used-for","object":"dynamical model"},{"question_id":null,"document_id":"scierc:train:7","subject"